1. FinReason Cup Task 1 data (CC BY 4.0)
----------------------------------------

Source:  FinReason Cup Task 1 release, dataset version task1-v4
         (test release identity task1-v4-test-questions-20260901-r2),
         published by the FinReason Cup organizers (public display name
         The Fin AI).
         Canonical URL: https://the-finai.github.io/IEEE-bigdata-cup/task1/
         Repository:    https://github.com/The-FinAI/IEEE-bigdata-cup
                        (public/task1/data/)
Licence: Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International
         https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
         (scope: the exact data allowlist in the cup's
         public/task1/RIGHTS_AND_PROVENANCE.md)

This product does not contain or redistribute the data files. The pipeline
reads them in place from the user's own checkout of the repository above.

Changes were made. The solver artifacts in artifacts/, the reports in
docs/results/ and some test fixtures are derived from the train, dev,
leaderboard and test files of the release. Gold values come from the train and
dev files only. The leaderboard and test questions carry no labels; they were
used for the shape-coverage and hidden-stratum counts in the measurement
reports, for the test fixtures, and to select which search-fitted components
stage 5 replaces. shape_index.json holds the question wording with every
number masked, keyed to template ids; fallback_model.json holds tokenised
trace-slot descriptions, template ids and majority enum labels; solvers.json
holds template ids and the arithmetic programs fitted to the gold values;
dev_predictions.jsonl holds dev case ids with the values this product computes
for them; the measurement reports hold case ids, input-file sha256 digests and
aggregate counts; tests/ holds a few leaderboard and test case ids with
phrases from their questions and values worked out by hand from that text.
Numbers were masked, text was tokenised, and gold values were replaced by
fitted programs and computed outputs.

The hidden-stratum forms in this bundle (src/finreason_solver/hiddenforms/
and hidden/forms/final/) are also derived from the test release: each form
quotes phrases of its template's test questions with the numbers masked and
computes the recipe the question states; routes.json holds the masked
question shapes. hidden/templates.json, which holds the test questions
verbatim, is not included. The workspace notes in hidden/README.md quote one
test question in full as a worked example, and some form docstrings quote the
numbers of individual draws; both come from the organizers' CC BY 4.0 release.


2. FinChain generator source (Apache-2.0)
-----------------------------------------

Source:  FinChain, https://github.com/mbzuai-nlp/finchain
         Copyright (c) FINCHAIN contributors.
Licence: Apache License, Version 2.0 (upstream LICENSE, added in upstream
         commit 9bd2942b85d992844b77094a8b822aa16832703c on 2026-09-17).

The closed forms in src/finreason_solver/genforms.py (stage 4) were
transcribed by reading the FinChain generator functions they name. The
reference forms in src/finreason_solver/refforms.py (stage 5) write down the
recipe stated in the cup's question text, with the FinChain generator read
alongside wherever the wording left a choice. The tree read is FinChain commit
146eaa8225bf867fe7386c2d4727b59e03170235, the commit pinned in the cup's
public/task1/RIGHTS_AND_PROVENANCE.md. That tree carries no licence file. The
licence commit 9bd2942b is its direct child and changes only LICENSE and
readme.md, so the source read is the source the upstream licence covers.

Changes were made: each computation was rewritten in this product's own
exact-arithmetic expression language over the numbers lifted from the cup's
question text, and where the cup's wording diverges from the generator, the
cup's gold values were followed. No FinChain file is included, no FinChain
code is executed, and FinChain is not an input to the build. Upstream ships no
NOTICE file.

Citation: Xie et al., "FinChain: A Symbolic Benchmark for Verifiable
Chain-of-Thought Financial Reasoning", ACL 2026, pp. 14529-14553,
doi:10.18653/v1/2026.acl-long.662.


